// The engine's own pixel shader (T0141, D26).
//
// **This file is why D26 exists.** DiligentFX's `RenderPBR.psh` is private and
// has no hook before texture sampling, so parallax, height mapping and triplanar
// are unreachable through it at any setting. Rather than patch a vendored
// submodule, the engine owns this half and calls DiligentFX's *public* shading
// library for the lighting -- surface properties in, radiance out.
//
// The split, and it is the same one Godot and Unreal draw:
//
//   surface stage (ours)  ->  read textures, displace UVs, blend layers
//   lighting     (theirs) ->  BRDF, punctual lights, IBL, shadows
//
// Included from DiligentFX, all public, none of it modified:
//   PBR_Structures.fxh   the shared constant-buffer layouts
//   PBR_Shading.fxh      the lighting functions (T0141.10)
//   Shadows.fxh, PCF.fxh shadow sampling and filtering (T0086)

#include "PBR_Structures.fxh"

// Grown deliberately rather than written whole: this compiles and renders
// before it does anything interesting, so that the *plumbing* -- our source
// reaching the compiler, and a DiligentFX public header resolving from it --
// is proven separately from the shading. A shader that compiles and draws the
// wrong thing is the failure T0134 spent a ticket on, and the two are much
// easier to tell apart when they land in different commits.

struct HpSurfaceProbeVSOutput
{
    float4 Pos : SV_POSITION;
};

/// Writes the missing-material colour (T0060.10): magenta, unlit.
///
/// Deliberately the *fallback* colour rather than an arbitrary one, so that the
/// first thing this shader ever draws is a value the engine already gives a
/// meaning to, and a test asserting it is asserting something real.
float4 main(in HpSurfaceProbeVSOutput In) : SV_TARGET
{
    return float4(1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0);
}
